id: decisions/030-first-move-conformance
title: "Whether the first .yawn must conform"
kind: decision-frame
record_shape: header
status: draft
authored_by: agent-on-behalf
authored_via: "Claude Code session on Dave's behalf, 2026-09-12; drafted from the JUDGMENTS.md reversal axis 'onboarding-friction'; recommendation is an attributed inference and ratifies nothing"
version: "1.0.0"
updated: 2026-09-12
template_ref: templates/decision.yawn
principal_ref: principal:dave

axis: onboarding-friction
axis_gloss: "whether the first move is made as cheap as possible, or the newcomer's first artifact must already conform to the corpus's rules"
poles:
  a: low-friction-entry
  b: conformance-at-entry
split: lone-exception
draft_status: full
leverage: 8.6
leverage_formula: "judgments_resolved(18) x split_weight(0.4) x authored_conflict_bonus(1.2)"

question: >
  Must a newcomer's first .yawn file already conform to the corpus (id, kind,
  status, proof), or is the first move allowed to be non-conforming and repaired
  later?

why_it_matters: >
  yawn.yawn tells a newcomer to copy templates/basic.yawn, and that file lacks
  the three keys nearly every other record has, so the cheapest first move
  produces the least conforming record. Once answered, a bot that ingests
  newcomer files knows whether to expect the spine or to add it, and a child
  Yawn started from basic.yawn inherits either a conforming record or a debt.

state_snapshot:
  description: >
    17 judgments sit at low-friction-entry (7 authored) and 1 at
    conformance-at-entry (0 authored). The repository asserts both directions on
    this axis.
  attributed_to: TAXONOMY.md
  epistemic_status: observed
  source_refs:
    - JUDGMENTS.md
    - TAXONOMY.md

lacuna:
  kind: unselected_choice
  description: >
    Whether Dave meant readme.yawn-before-README.md as a deliberate ranking of his
    own format over Markdown, or as an unordered list.

options:
  - name: A
    description: >
      rule: The first move is as cheap as possible. templates/basic.yawn stays
      minimal (title, signal, inference, current, possible, lacuna, boundary, move,
      proof, replay, source). Conformance keys are added when the record is next
      touched, never demanded at entry.
    cost: "None now; every newcomer file starts non-conforming and a later sweep or per-touch repair is owed."
    risk: "Records made from basic.yawn cannot be validated, ranked or linked by id until someone repairs them."
    reversibility: true
    proof_needed: "None enforces this; the record is a note in yawn.yawn first_move that conformance comes later."
    evidence_for:
      - "J0026"
      - "J0159"
      - "J0266"
      - "J0378"
      - "J0482"
      - "J0542"
  - name: B
    description: >
      rule: The first artifact must conform. templates/basic.yawn carries id, kind,
      status and proof, and the newcomer is told what each is before writing.
    cost: "The front door starts explaining id/kind/status; contradicts the authored front_door_rule, must_not_force and questions/node.yawn purpose."
    risk: "The failure the repo names in core/RELATIONSHIP_FIRST_AGENT_ARENA.yawn 18.8 (ontology before value) is reintroduced at the door."
    reversibility: true
    proof_needed: "The record-spine test applied to templates/basic.yawn plus README copy that teaches the keys."
    evidence_for:
      - "J0125"
  - name: C
    description: >
      rule: The first move stays cheap in prose: one natural question, no ontology
      first, no helper copy. But templates/basic.yawn pre-fills the spine keys (id,
      kind, status) with placeholder values, so the file a newcomer produces
      conforms without the newcomer being taught why. core/yawn-space.yawn
      minimal_shape either declares itself ordered or lists README.md first.
    cost: "Three lines added to templates/basic.yawn; one wording change in core/yawn-space.yawn."
    risk: "Placeholder ids collide or are never replaced."
    reversibility: true
    proof_needed: "The record-spine test (see record-shape-uniformity) run against templates/basic.yawn; a check in tests/ that yawn.yawn first_move points at a template that passes it."
    evidence_for:
      - "J0026"
      - "J0378"
      - "J0482"
      - "J0542"

recommendation:
  option_ref: C
  confidence: 0.7
  epistemic_status: inferred
  authority: none
  reasoning: >
    Seven authored judgments say the reader must not be made to learn the ontology
    first; none of them says the file may be non-conforming. The single opposing
    judgment, J0125, is revealed from a list order that is never called ordered,
    and is better removed as evidence than written as a rule. C keeps every
    authored low-friction rule and closes the gap GROUND_TRUTH FACT 13 names:
    onboarding was ranked above conformance silently. It depends on the
    record-spine decision for what the placeholders are.

target_condition:
  mode: decide
  proposed_by: agent-on-behalf
  affected_principals:
    - principal:dave
  ratification_status: proposed
  sufficient_when:
    - "templates/basic.yawn carries id, kind and status with placeholder values and passes the record-spine test."
    - "questions/node.yawn and interface/new-yawn-v0.1.yawn still open with a natural question and no taxonomy headers."
    - "core/yawn-space.yawn minimal_shape either says it is ordered or lists README.md before readme.yawn."

choice:
  selected_by: ""
  option_ref: ""
  authority_ref: ""

proof:
  covers:
    - target_condition
    - options
  falsifier: >
    basic.yawn gains the keys but the first README screen starts by explaining id,
    kind and status.
  status: proposed

evidence_sample:
  - "J0026"
  - "J0159"
  - "J0266"
  - "J0378"
  - "J0482"
  - "J0542"
  - "J0125"

boundary:
  - "confidence cannot ratify an objective; this record proposes and Dave decides"
  - "the REVEALED judgments cited here describe what artifacts do, not what Dave chose"

source:
  template: templates/decision.yawn
  axis: TAXONOMY.md
  inventory: JUDGMENTS.md
  id_index: TAXONOMY.md
